00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:38.290
Every time we see diatoms, we have to give&nbsp;
it to them: they’re just simply stunning.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.659
They’re single-celled and major&nbsp;
producers of the oxygen we breathe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.659 --> 00:00:47.720
but the real reason we love seeing&nbsp;
them is because of their frustules—

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.681
those silica shells they construct&nbsp;
for themselves to live in.

00:00:51.681 --> 00:00:58.298
They’re architects building stained glass&nbsp;
walls to float in, and the effect is beautiful.

00:01:00.101 --> 00:01:07.406
But apparently, diatoms are not content&nbsp;
to rest on their ornate laurels.

00:01:07.406 --> 00:01:12.000
Because some don’t just&nbsp;
construct a home for themselves.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
They construct a much larger structure&nbsp;
that houses a whole gathering of diatoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.020
an apartment building made of one long tube.

00:01:23.559 --> 00:01:25.734
Yes, a tube.

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:33.506
They’re really striking to look at, like pieces&nbsp;
of hay or strands of hair braided together.

00:01:33.506 --> 00:01:37.111
The tubes we’re looking at&nbsp;
right now come from Iceland.

00:01:37.111 --> 00:01:42.823
Our master of microscopes James sent his&nbsp;
assistant Ela there on a microbe hunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.823 --> 00:01:46.228
collecting samples from a&nbsp;
bunch of different places.

00:01:46.228 --> 00:01:49.744
But they saved the marine samples for last because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.744 --> 00:01:54.120
those organisms tend to be&nbsp;
more delicate to handle.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.827
So on this last collecting session,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.827 --> 00:02:02.560
James was on a video call, watching as&nbsp;
Ela scanned the dock she was standing on.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.519
And along the submerged parts of the dock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.519 --> 00:02:11.048
James spotted tufts of brownish&nbsp;
stuff peeking out as the waves broke.

00:02:11.048 --> 00:02:14.816
So he asked Ela to grab some and bring them back.

00:02:14.816 --> 00:02:20.418
The next day, she arrived at 6 am&nbsp;
and crashed on the couch, while James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.418 --> 00:02:26.748
immediately grabbed the samples to take a look.&nbsp;
And apparently, the entire time, he was saying,

00:02:26.748 --> 00:02:28.814
“This is so beautiful.”

00:02:28.814 --> 00:02:33.888
The samples that Ela brought back from&nbsp;
the dock were teeming with diatoms.

00:02:33.888 --> 00:02:39.866
It might seem surprising that the waters&nbsp;
of Iceland can bring forth so much life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.866 --> 00:02:43.924
especially during the cold and dark winter months.

00:02:43.924 --> 00:02:50.597
But this matches James’ experience with the&nbsp;
ponds in Poland he gathers samples from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.597 --> 00:02:55.399
which often end up dominated&nbsp;
by diatoms during the winter—

00:02:55.399 --> 00:02:58.293
even as green algae numbers drop.

00:02:58.293 --> 00:03:02.207
Diatoms seem to be fairly hardy in the dark.

00:03:02.207 --> 00:03:08.869
One study found that diatoms survive longer than&nbsp;
flagellates in extended periods of darkness.

00:03:08.869 --> 00:03:15.780
The researchers didn’t find a specific reason why&nbsp;
they’re able to tolerate the dark for so long, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:22.759
they theorized that perhaps the diatoms were more&nbsp;
conservative in how they managed energy reserves.

00:03:22.759 --> 00:03:27.660
Others have also suggested that their&nbsp;
survival may be due to oils stored in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:33.448
the diatoms, which may keep them better&nbsp;
stocked compared to other organisms.

00:03:33.448 --> 00:03:38.401
Their survival in winter means&nbsp;
that when spring rolls around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.401 --> 00:03:43.794
diatoms are often the first&nbsp;
groups to bloom in polar waters.

00:03:43.794 --> 00:03:48.813
And perhaps these tube-like&nbsp;
structures are part of their success.

00:03:48.813 --> 00:03:55.591
They’re not found as often in warmer waters,&nbsp;
but things are different when it gets cold.

00:03:55.591 --> 00:04:03.494
In 1994, a scientist found a whole bunch of&nbsp;
these tubes along the Dutch coast in late winter.

00:04:03.494 --> 00:04:06.900
And by a whole bunch, I mean there were more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:12.800
10 billion diatom cells living&nbsp;
in tubes, per square meter.

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:20.820
These tubular colonies start with&nbsp;
one diatom secreting a thread of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:24.548
mucus from a pore, which it weaves into a tube.

00:04:24.548 --> 00:04:30.720
Living in a mucus tube might not have&nbsp;
the same romantic feel as the diatom’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:37.782
stained glass shells, but diatoms&nbsp;
don’t have much need for romance.

00:04:37.782 --> 00:04:45.480
As the tube gets longer and longer, the cell&nbsp;
reproduces asexually, dividing and populating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:52.344
the tube with more cells like it so they can&nbsp;
travel through their shared home and add to it.

00:04:52.344 --> 00:04:57.659
With every cell, a new roommate&nbsp;
and construction worker is added,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.659 --> 00:05:03.760
a cell that can strengthen and add&nbsp;
to the tube by producing more mucus.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.435
At times, the cells might reproduce&nbsp;
faster than the tube can expand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.435 --> 00:05:13.778
at which point they might start&nbsp;
to create a little side tunnel.

00:05:13.778 --> 00:05:18.540
Their tube is a colony, and they&nbsp;
might end up attached to surfaces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:23.480
like sand particles, or a submerged Iceland dock.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:27.675
And they’re kind of similar to&nbsp;
colonies we’ve seen on this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.675 --> 00:05:31.320
like Volvox and their spherical gatherings.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:37.801
One thing that’s strange though is that based&nbsp;
on what little we know about these tubes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:43.614
it doesn’t seem as if these diatoms&nbsp;
do much to coordinate their activity.

00:05:43.614 --> 00:05:49.466
This is different from volvox, where&nbsp;
various cells take on different functions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.466 --> 00:05:53.818
and they work together to create&nbsp;
a better life for everyone.

00:05:53.818 --> 00:06:00.180
But some of these diatoms act like they’ve&nbsp;
constructed a crowded city street just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:06.771
ignore each other, a group of co-workers who&nbsp;
don’t even say hi to each other in the hallway.

00:06:06.771 --> 00:06:12.418
They’re little independent entities,&nbsp;
countless ships passing in the night.

00:06:12.418 --> 00:06:17.657
And for those living in narrower&nbsp;
tubes, there’s no movement at all.

00:06:17.657 --> 00:06:23.414
They’re simply stuck in place, next to&nbsp;
a family member they don’t acknowledge.

00:06:23.414 --> 00:06:28.157
But there must be a reason they&nbsp;
live together in these tubes.

00:06:28.157 --> 00:06:34.116
Otherwise, why bother with the time and&nbsp;
energy it takes to start making a tube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.116 --> 00:06:36.407
and to keep adding to it.

00:06:36.407 --> 00:06:39.728
The tube might be a form of protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.728 --> 00:06:45.000
making the diatoms too big for&nbsp;
microbial predators to consume.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:52.277
But it’s probably also a way to help the diatoms&nbsp;
access light through a few different mechanisms.

00:06:52.277 --> 00:06:58.106
To start, the tubes help lift the diatoms away&nbsp;
from the substrate they might have sunk to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.106 --> 00:07:02.225
raising them towards any light&nbsp;
that might penetrate the water.

00:07:02.225 --> 00:07:06.960
And as they float in the water,&nbsp;
the tubes allow the diatoms to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:12.559
move with the rhythm of the waves,&nbsp;
illuminating all sides in the process.

00:07:12.559 --> 00:07:18.882
At least, that’s one of the possibilities&nbsp;
for why these diatoms turn to tubes.

00:07:18.882 --> 00:07:26.057
But stepping back from the “why,” it’s like&nbsp;
we’re starting to see more of who diatoms are.

00:07:26.057 --> 00:07:27.762
They’re builders.

00:07:27.762 --> 00:07:30.862
They may never have set out to become that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.862 --> 00:07:36.000
but their approach to the world around&nbsp;
them has been to build, to assemble.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.538
To make something functional and beautiful&nbsp;
even if the beauty is not the point.

